    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Dodge Charger Gets Special-Edition Package Celebrating 90 Years of Mopar\nWorld’s First Dual-Certified Flying Car Gears Up for Large-Scale Production\n9 photos\nPhoto: Pal-V\nDutch company Pal-V is at the forefront of what it calls an entirely new category of mobility for both private owners and professionals across multiple sectors. Its flagship product, the radical Liberty flying car has recently secured dual (automotive and aviation) certification, which now moves the focus on the next stage, that of industrial manufacturing.\nWhile the brand-new Tesla Roadster 2 has everyone talking and dreaming about a Sci-Fi future coming to life, this European vehicle continues steadily on the road to global production. It’s not as flashy or infamous as the Roadster 2, but it’s fully certified – both on the road and in the sky.\nAlso, this shouldn’t necessarily be seen as a competition, as these two flying cars are vastly different, and the mechanisms behind their ability to “fly” are completely different as well.\nPal-V’s Liberty is based on the FlyDrive technology that combines aircraft and car design. It’s basically a gyroplane integrated into a conventional car, with blades that act as rotors. These blades are powerful enough to generate lift, so that the car can “fly”, but their lightweight and foldable design also makes them invisible during drive mode.\nThe Liberty can switch from fly mode to drive mode in a matter of minutes, and is overall compact enough to allows easy parking and handling. On the road, it’s designed for an 817-mile (1,315 km) range without refueling. In the sky, it flies safely at 11,000 feet (3,350 meters).\nAs of this year, the Liberty is officially certified, making Pal-V the first company in the FlyDrive sector to secure both automotive and aviation production approvals. With the focus now shifting to large-scale production, the Dutch company has joined forces with Nobleo Manufacturing specifically for the industrialization phase.\nThe partnership will address all phases from pre-production to production ramp-up and then large-scale manufacturing, with the goal of creating a clear roadmap for future expansion. Pal-V says that its reservations portfolio for the Liberty flying car is already big enough to support the first few years of production.\nAlthough this Dutch flying car became famous primarily for its appeal to private owners, FlyDrive vehicles are generally intended to do much more across multiple industries. They are a combination of cars and eVTOLs (electric vertical takeoff and landing aircraft) with tremendous potential for critical transportation in areas with difficult transportation conditions.\nThe ability to switch seamlessly from drive mode to fly mode is not about showing off and having more fun on the road (although that’s part of it to some extent), but about providing an alternative that can improve accessibility dramatically. In Pal-V’s vision, FlyDrive vehicles are just one part of a new era of mobility that will focus heavily on eVTOLs, across cargo, passenger transportation, and autonomous public service shuttles in the sky.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nflying car PAL-V Liberty flying car Dutch flying car FlyDrive\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT 5 Underrated Performance Models From Europe's Diesel Golden Era",
